# =============================================================================
# MoEStream configuration
#
#   The short version: set MODEL_DIR, then run `make launch`.
#
#   The launcher reads every GGUF's header, reads this machine's memory limits,
#   and asks only what it cannot work out for itself -- which model, how many
#   concurrent requests, how much context, and, when the model would fit whole,
#   whether you would rather have the memory or the speed. Everything else (MoE
#   or dense, slot count, arena size, thresholds, read threads, sampling
#   defaults) is derived at run time and deliberately has no setting here.
#
#   Prefer to run it yourself? `docker compose up -d` still reads this file, and
#   the settings below still work. The launcher just fills them in for you.
# =============================================================================

# --- the two things the launcher still needs ---------------------------------
MODEL_DIR=/path/to/your/models      # a directory on the host, mounted read-only
MS_PORT=8091                        # the port to publish

# --- everything below is optional ---------------------------------------------
#   `make launch` writes these into .env.launcher. Set them here only if you are
#   running docker compose directly, or want to override what the launcher picks.
#
#   MODEL_FILE            for a split GGUF, name the first shard
#   CTX_SIZE              context length
#   N_PARALLEL            concurrent requests. On a dense model this is the
#                         single biggest lever: streaming costs ~3x at 1 and
#                         ~1.2x at 16 (docs/findings/S37)
#   UBATCH                learn | a number. Prompt processing is free above 1024
#                         and expensive below it on a streamed dense model
#   MOESTREAM_CACHE_FRAC  MoE only.  learn | auto | a fraction
#   MOESTREAM_DENSE_FRAC  dense only. auto | learn | off | a fraction
#

# =============================================================================
# Advanced — you do not need any of this to run the server
#
#   `make launch` sets what matters and derives the rest. What follows documents
#   the settings behind that, for running `docker compose up -d` by hand or for
#   overriding a specific choice. Every default here is the one measurement
#   supports.
#
#   Derived automatically, and therefore absent on purpose -- a value left here
#   after swapping models silently applies the previous model's settings:
#     MoE or dense      from the GGUF's expert count
#     top_k             from GGUF metadata
#     slab slot count   MOESTREAM_CACHE_FRAC x the model's expert count
#     arena size        from one layer's expert bytes
#     arena threshold   largest ubatch the slab can serve without exhaustion
#     sampling          the model's own defaults from the GGUF
#     thread count      the host's physical core count
#     dense I/O threads fixed at 4 (docs/RESULTS.md 10.12)
#     BATCH             raised to at least UBATCH
#     render/video GID  detected by `make up`
#
#   What was actually chosen is printed at startup:
#     docker logs moestream 2>&1 | grep -a moestream
# =============================================================================
#
# MODEL_FILE -- the GGUF to serve. MoE or dense; MoEStream reads the header and
# takes the right path on its own. For a split GGUF, name the first shard.
#MODEL_FILE=your-model.gguf

# =============================================================================
# 2. TUNING — the values that actually matter
# =============================================================================

# ---- how much of the model stays resident -----------------------------------
#   This alone sets the memory/speed trade-off.
#   slots = this value x the model's expert count (computed automatically):
#     256-expert model -> 0.25 gives  64 slots
#     512-expert model -> 0.25 gives 128 slots
#
#   Measured on Ornith-35B-IQ4_NL (device memory / decode, 2026-08-06):
#     1.00 -> 17.29 GiB / 41.5 ms   (equivalent to no streaming)
#     0.60 -> ~11    GiB / 53.1 ms  (above 0.60 it gets slower again)
#     0.25 ->  7.44 GiB / 58.7 ms
#     0.15 ->  4.6  GiB / 90.6 ms
#
#   Verified across three models (docs/RESULTS.md §12.5):
#     Ornith-35B (256E)        0.25 ->  64 slots /  7.44 GiB / 17.0 tok/s
#     Qwen3-Coder-Next (512E)  0.25 -> 128 slots / 13.15 GiB /  9.8 tok/s
#     Laguna-S-2.1 (256E)      0.20 ->  51 slots / 18.51 GiB /  3.1 tok/s
#
#   ** learn ** -- recommended. Records what your own traffic wants into
#   ./state/tuning.tsv and applies it on the next start. The best slot count
#   depends on the workload, not only the model: the same model measured 0.25
#   for short replies and 0.40 for long-form generation (§10.9).
#     - keyed by model identity, so swapping models never misapplies it
#     - capped by free device memory, so it never recommends a value that
#       fails to start (headroom: MOESTREAM_MEM_RESERVE_GIB, default 1.0)
#     - the first run starts conservatively at 0.15 and converges upward
#     - needs about 625 generated tokens of ordinary use before it can decide
#       (the measurement counts decode only; prefill goes through the arena)
#
#   ** auto ** -- size the slab by a memory budget instead, from
#   MOESTREAM_SLAB_GIB (default 9.0). Use when memory is the hard constraint.
#
#   You can also measure it yourself without restarting repeatedly: reuse
#   distance is independent of cache size, so one session yields the hit rate
#   for every possible slot count.
#       docker kill -s USR1 moestream
#       docker logs moestream 2>&1 | grep -a '\[mrc\]'
#   See docs/USAGE.md §4.5.
MOESTREAM_CACHE_FRAC=learn

# ---- micro-batch: the main knob for prefill speed ----------------------------
#   Larger chunks re-read the experts fewer times, but per-token compute also
#   rises with chunk size because attention is quadratic within a micro-batch.
#   Those two effects balance at an optimum that is strongly model-dependent,
#   and they move in opposite directions -- higher is not always better:
#
#     Ornith-35B (ctx=32768,     Qwen3-Coder-Next (frac=0.20,   Laguna-S-2.1
#      2.5k-token prompts)        23k-token prompts)             (ctx=100000)
#        512 -> 212.1 tok/s         1024 -> 114.4 tok/s           1024 ->  80.1
#       1024 -> 252.7 tok/s <-peak  2048 -> 128.1 tok/s           8096 -> 116.0 <-peak
#       2048 -> 226.8 tok/s (-10%)  4096 -> 136.3 tok/s <- peak
#       4096 -> 211.7 tok/s (-16%)  8192 -> 135.2 tok/s
#
#   The peak sits at a different place in each: there is no value to copy.
#
#   The optimum cannot be extrapolated from a single run (§10.11), so `learn`
#   tries each candidate once and then keeps the fastest, recording results in
#   ./state/ubatch.tsv -- four restarts to finish the search. Set a number to
#   pin it.
#     candidates: 1024 2048 4096 8192  (override with UBATCH_CANDIDATES)
#
#   Measured per request -- prompt tokens divided by prefill time, the same
#   quantity llama.cpp reports as "prompt eval" -- so the number means the same
#   thing at every ubatch. Verified against llama.cpp on Qwen3-Coder: 0.02% to
#   0.55% apart across all four candidates, same winner (§10.16).
#   Needs two completed requests per start; prompt length does not matter.
#
#   Each measurement is recorded against the frac it was taken at, because the
#   two interact: a larger slab misses less, which weakens the reason to use a
#   large ubatch. On Ornith the best ubatch is 1024 at frac=0.25 but 2048 at
#   frac=0.15 (§10.16). When frac moves, the old rows stop matching and the
#   candidates are measured again -- so leave frac to settle first if you are
#   setting both to `learn`.
UBATCH=learn

# ---- context length ---------------------------------------------------------
#   KV cache memory scales with this, and a longer context also makes a
#   prompt-cache miss more expensive to recover from.
CTX_SIZE=32768


# ---- which llama.cpp to patch -----------------------------------------------
#   MoEStream is a patch against llama.cpp internals -- llama-graph.cpp,
#   llama-model-loader.cpp, ggml-vulkan.cpp -- which are not a public API and
#   change often upstream.
#
#   The default is the most recent upstream commit this project has been built
#   and verified against (2026-08-23). The numbers in docs/RESULTS.md were
#   measured on 3581ba0cf (2026-08-02); an alternating A/B put the two within
#   1.2% on decode, with the newer one using 0.51 GiB more -- a difference that
#   appears with MOESTREAM=0 as well, so it is upstream's, not this patch's.
#   Leave it alone unless you have a reason.
#
#   To follow upstream instead:
#       LLAMA_COMMIT=master
#   Then rebuild with `docker compose build --no-cache`. If upstream moved the
#   code the patch anchors point at, src/apply.py stops the build with a named
#   assertion rather than producing a silently wrong binary -- so a failure here
#   is loud, not subtle. Report it as an issue; it is fixable, but it needs the
#   anchor updating.
#
#   Whether a newer llama.cpp is faster is not a given: the numbers in
#   docs/RESULTS.md hold for the pinned commit only.
# --- dense models -----------------------------------------------------------
# MoEStream reads the GGUF and takes the expert path or the dense path on its
# own; nothing here needs setting for a normal run.
#
#   auto   (default) stream the least of a dense model's FFN that still lets it
#          fit. A model that already fits streams nothing and behaves exactly
#          like plain llama.cpp.
#   learn  as auto, and report the measured cost per GiB on this machine.
#          Note this is NOT the expert cache's kind of learning: dense cost is
#          linear in bytes moved out, so there is no knee to search for.
#   off    never stream a dense model.
#   <0..1> keep this fraction of layers resident.
#
# Prompt processing is free above UBATCH=1024 and expensive below it; generation
# costs about 3.2x with the whole FFN streamed. Details: docs/findings/S27.
#MOESTREAM_DENSE_FRAC=auto

LLAMA_COMMIT=b0539c43ed13b16bf0d8a0840646faea65469702


# =============================================================================
# 3. PREFERENCE
# =============================================================================
# Prompt cache in host RAM (MiB). This dominates perceived responsiveness:
# when it hits, tokens evaluated on the second and later turns drop by more
# than 100x. llama.cpp's default of 8192 is too large for a 32 GB machine.
# 0 disables it.
CACHE_RAM=2048

# KV quantization. q8_0 balances quality and size; q4_0 saves memory.
CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0

# Context checkpoints; makes branching and rewinding a conversation faster.
CTX_CHECKPOINTS=4

# Thinking mode. false answers directly, which suits agents and tool use.
ENABLE_THINKING=false
# How reasoning is returned (auto / none / deepseek)
REASONING_FORMAT=auto

# Serve llama.cpp's bundled Web UI (0 disables)
ENABLE_WEBUI=1

# Concurrent requests.
#   Measured (Ornith): 2 requests take 18.6 s sequentially, 13.0 s concurrently
#   (1.43x throughput). A single agent issues no concurrent requests, so this
#   makes no difference there; raise it only if you actually run several.
#   Note: concurrency changes batch composition, so generated tokens can differ
#   from the sequential case. That is stock llama.cpp behaviour.
N_PARALLEL=1

# Optional API key (no authentication when empty)
# API_KEY=

# Optional: override the chat template (path inside the container)
# CHAT_TEMPLATE=/models/chat_template.jinja

# Sampling defaults to the model's own values from the GGUF.
#   Recommended settings differ per model, so pinning values here will misapply
#   them after a model swap. Uncomment only to override deliberately.
# TEMP=0.6
# TOP_P=0.95
# TOP_K=20
# MIN_P=0.0
# PRESENCE_PENALTY=0.0

# Extra arguments passed straight through to llama-server
# EXTRA_ARGS=


# =============================================================================
# 4. LEAVE ALONE
#    Each of these has one setting that measurement supports. They are listed
#    so the defaults are visible, not because they invite tuning.
# =============================================================================
#   MOESTREAM=1                    0 runs as plain llama.cpp. `make launch`
#                                  writes 0 here if you choose speed over memory
#                                  on a model that fits whole
#   MOESTREAM_PREFILL_ARENA=1      0 makes prefill 5.3x slower and forces you to
#                                  keep MOESTREAM_MAX_UBATCH in sync by hand
#   N_GPU_LAYERS=99                anything less is untested; partial offload
#                                  breaks the slab's assumptions
#   FLASH_ATTN=on                  no measured reason to choose otherwise
#   RENDER_GID / VIDEO_GID         detected by `make up`. Set them only if you
#                                  run `docker compose up -d` directly and the
#                                  defaults (992 / 44) are wrong on your host:
#                                    getent group render | cut -d: -f3
#                                    getent group video  | cut -d: -f3
#
#   Speculative decoding. `make launch` sets this for you: it asks llama.cpp
#   whether the model can self-speculate (moestream-spec-probe, which calls
#   upstream's own common_speculative_types_from_gguf) and enables it only where
#   measurement says it pays. `learn` is the setting the launcher writes: it asks
#   llama.cpp whether the model can self-speculate at all, then tries one draft
#   size per start from `off 1 2 3 5` and keeps the fastest, judged by the
#   server's own generation throughput so rejected drafts are already subtracted.
#   Measured: 2.29x on a streamed dense model, break-even to a 65% loss on a MoE
#   one depending on the arguments, and nothing at all for the model-free ngram-*
#   kinds (docs/findings/S42). Pin a literal flag string here to override.
#   SPEC_DECODING=learn
#   SPEC_CANDIDATES="off 1 2 3 5"   # what learn tries
#   SPEC_P_MIN=0.7                  # stop drafting when the head is unsure
#   SPEC_MIN_TOKENS=200             # generated tokens before a row is recorded


# =============================================================================
# Diagnostics — for investigating, not for tuning
# =============================================================================
#   MOESTREAM_DEBUG=1             show startup self-checks (silent when they pass)
#   MOESTREAM_MEM_RESERVE_GIB=1.0 device memory left free when capping a recommendation
#   MOESTREAM_STATE_DIR=/state    where learned tuning is kept
#   MOESTREAM_SLAB_GIB=9.0        slab budget when MOESTREAM_CACHE_FRAC=auto
#   MOESTREAM_IO_THREADS=N        pin the I/O thread count (default: auto-tuned)
#   MOESTREAM_ZEROCOPY=0          disable direct pread on UMA (default 1)
#   MOESTREAM_PREFILL_ASYNC=0     disable async arena prefetch (default 1)
#   MOESTREAM_PREFILL_BUFS=1      arena count; 1 saves 498 MiB, costs 9.7% prefill
#   MOESTREAM_PREFILL_THRESHOLD=N set the arena switch threshold (default: auto)
#   MOESTREAM_MAX_UBATCH=N        lower bound on slab size (default 2)
#   MOESTREAM_MRC=0               disable reuse-distance measurement (0.3% overhead)
#   MOESTREAM_PT_PER_GIB=2.5      threshold for the slot recommendation
#   MOESTREAM_PROBE_HIDDEN=1      measure what a layer-lookahead predictor would cost
#   MOESTREAM_NOOP=1|3            1 = pass the remap through / 3 = cache lookup only
#   MOESTREAM_FORCE_PASSES=N      Expert Sweep experiment; disabled by default
#   MOESTREAM_DROP_FROM=N         from layer N up, treat misses as the null expert
#   MOESTREAM_ORDER_TRACE=1       log the graph execution order
